  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4({sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, we have to use the identity $1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x.$ Rearranging this gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4({sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
